  "narrative": "The new panel strongly supports the remaining accepted but unvalidated TP53-USP7 association and independently confirms the PTEN-STAT3 association in a second TP53 context. For USP7, the claim direction is +1, so a positive signed estimate means TP53-loss cells have a less negative, weaker USP7 dependency score. H16 in adenocarcinoma gives +0.7990 [0.7755, 0.8225], H44 in Avana screens gives +0.8057 [0.7851, 0.8263], H73 in high-quality screens with screen_roc_auc at least 0.93 gives +0.8079 [0.7855, 0.8304], and H50 in non-Avana screens gives +0.7674 [0.7031, 0.8318]. All four are far above the 0.1 practical benchmark and have intervals excluding zero. H50 is less precise because only 43 exposed lines contribute, but the lower bound remains extremely large, so I accept it while treating it as a smaller, noisier robustness check. These context-specific results are internally consistent with the rejected library and screen-quality interactions: the association is not explained by one major library or screen-quality stratum. H7 remains the largest accepted unvalidated association, and the convergence across technical and biological contexts makes independent validation of H7 now the highest-value validation request.\n\nPTEN loss within TP53-wildtype cells, H96, gives signed +0.2870 [0.2273, 0.3467] for dependency_STAT3 with direction -1. Because the direction is -1, the raw contrast is negative, meaning PTEN-loss TP53-wildtype lines have a more negative STAT3 dependency score, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. This is clinically meaningful, excludes zero, and complements the independently validated overall PTEN-STAT3 result H67 and the TP53-loss subgroup H97. The pattern supports a broad dataset-level PTEN-STAT3 association rather than a TP53-context artifact. I accept and close H96.\n\nThe newly activated specificity controls are null. STK11 loss and STAT3 dependency is +0.0145 [-0.0137, 0.0427] (H111), SMAD4 loss and STAT3 is +0.0109 [-0.0257, 0.0475] (H112), MSI-high and DHX9 is +0.0172 [-0.0174, 0.0517] (H113), MSI-high and POLR2A is -0.0035 [-0.0348, 0.0277] (H114), and KRAS mutation and STAT3 is -0.0175 [-0.0418, 0.0068] (H115). All have absolute estimates far below the 0.1 benchmark and intervals compatible with no effect; the KRAS estimate also points away from the claimed stronger-dependency direction. I reject all five and close them. These results argue against a broad STAT3 co-mutational or RAS-pathway pattern in this dataset and support WRN-specificity of the MSI-high effect, since MSI was not associated with DHX9 or POLR2A.\n\nFinally, the high-quality-screen robustness checks for validated associations are clear. H74 shows SMARCA4 loss remains associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, signed +0.7895 [0.7676, 0.8114]; H75 shows MSI-high remains associated with stronger WRN dependency, signed +0.7924 [0.7599, 0.8249]. Both are accepted and closed as dataset-level consistency checks. Overall, the evidence favors a focused set of robust associations: TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. Multiplicity is mitigated by consistency across independent contexts and by the null specificity controls. I request validation of H7 to address the largest accepted unvalidated association. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
